Aligned priests have three attacks per round:

-- 4d10 plus the wielded weapon (mace, generally)
-- 1d4 kick (ooh, scary)
-- Clerical spellcast, which could be any of these damage spells:
   = Open Wounds for (#d6), where # is half the priest's level
	= Geyser (8d6)
	= Fire Pillar (8d6 and fire resistance protects you)
	= Lightning (8d6, shock resistance protects you)
	... and it could also be another paralysis spell.

So with AC-22, you'll average 11 points damage reduction and 4d10 will
average about 21 points of damage; the AC won't affect the spells he
casts on you, so that's an average of 10HP lost per turn period, and
could be as many as 40 if you're unlucky.  Priests cannot cast every
turn, but the ones that do hit will average 24HP lost.

For a 120HP character and assuming the priest casts every 4 turns, he
could very easily be dead in 8 turns:

Turns 1, 2, 3: bonk with the mace for 10, character at 90
Turn 4: bonk with the mace for 10, Open Wounds for 24, character at 56
Turns 5, 6, 7: bonk with the mace for 10, character at 26
Turn 8: bonk with the mace for 10, any damage spell

Now, does this mean the above scenario is _guaranteed_?  Of course not.
He could waste all his time with "Scales cover your eyes!", summon insects,  
and that sort of dreck.  But do you really want to leave your character
totally in the hands of the RNG?  Alternately, if you come out of 
paralysis with only 30hp remaining, how do you like your chances
against the full-health priest now?

If you have free action or magic resistance, it's a lot easier to
put up with the attrition since you can always step out of melee range
and it's assumed that you're actually hitting back.  But as long as
you're vulnerable to the paralysis spell, it's playing with fire to
tangle with aligned priests in melee.

